+Appendix~\ref{A:Sensitivity} varies attack rates from the base case of
+50\% to 30\% and 70\%, holding the epidemic peak constant at around
+three months; it also considers earlier and later epidemic peaks
+(two, four, or six months). Additional sensitivity analyses consider
+higher vaccine hesitancy rates (40\% or 70\% of each age cohort,
+compared to 20\% under the base case) and avoiding vaccination of
+recovered individuals, as well as a comparison using the age
+distribution of low-income countries. Each comparison uses the benchmark daily
+supply of 0.1\% and sigmoid dose response. The optimal uniform dose
+is higher when the population is younger: 57\% of a full dose under
+a population pyramid representative of low-income countries, compared
+with 50\% in high-income countries. In the other cases there is little
+variability in the optimal uniform dose, ranging from 48\% to 52\%.
+The additional relative and
+absolute benefits of optimising dose are more variable across these
+scenarios, since for some scenarios (high vaccine hesitancy or an
+earlier peak) vaccines are generally less effective at stopping the
+epidemic.
 
